eeded.  \\n\\nGiven opponents\\u2019 melds: Previous player has many W pungs, so W6 might be safer to discard (few players need it). T2 might be riskier because T1,T3 still in pool somewhat.  \\n\\nAlso, I must consider my own winning chances: If I discard B3 or B4, I lose chance for B2\\u2013B3\\u2013B4 chow. B2 left=1, so maybe keep B3,B4.  \\nThus discard **W6** seems reasonable.  \\n\\n**Reason:**  \\nW6 is isolated, no possible chow/pung in current tile availability (W7, W8 gone; W4,W5 already in exposed chow). Keeping B3,B4 retains possible B2\\u2013B3\\u2013B4 chow. Keeping T3 pair gives eye candidate. Discarding W6 is safe and doesn\\u2019t break potential melds in hand.  \\n\\n**Answer:** Play W6\"}"}
